// ===================== 1.4 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('1.4  Having been refused twice'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **Mark the ones where the person at the front of the sentence is not the person who acted, and mark them silently** — write a cross and say nothing until Stage 2. **In most classes three or four of the ten are broken**, and the owners will not know. **The silence matters: a student told in Stage 1 that their sentence is ungrammatical spends Stage 2 defending it instead of listening to a translator explain what it costs.**'));
out.push(A('**b** Correctly built: **2** and **4**. ⚠ **Broken: 1** (_the bolts_ did not read the specification), **3** (_the application_ was not refused — the applicant was), **5** (_the code_ did not wait eleven months). **Read 5 out last**: it is the one nobody spots, **because _having waited eleven months, the code finally appeared_ is a sentence most people have written and nobody has been corrected on.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **the participle has to belong to the subject of the main clause**, and when it does not, a reader cannot find who did it, and assumes you did not want them to.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **She changed the ask and not the argument.** **The second application had the history in the main clause** — _we are applying again because the need has not changed_ — **so the news was that she was applying again.** **The third put the history in front and left the main clause for the one new thing.** **2** **He sends about forty letters a term and has written _having considered the matter carefully, the start of the day will move_, or something like it, about forty times.** ⚠ **The start of the day considered nothing.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the only cycle in the unit whose central error is a grammatical mistake rather than a judgement, and it has to be taught through its consequence or it will not stick.** **Every class already knows, abstractly, that a participle needs a subject; three books of this course have never once made it matter.** **What makes it matter is Mrs Oueidat\'s two subjects.** **Put her example on the board in full:** _having reviewed the file, the application was declined_ — **and then ask the room to supply the missing subject.** **Somebody says _the committee_ and somebody says _the officer_**, and at that moment the class has seen that the English version declined to choose and that a reader will choose for it. ⚠ **Mr Tayara\'s forty letters are the second half of the lesson and the more uncomfortable one: a man has written this sentence forty times to four hundred parents and nobody has ever mentioned it.** **That is what an invisible fault is.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(refused twice)_ _**Having been refused twice on the same ground,** we have changed what we are asking for._ **Subject: we. We were refused. ✓**',
  '**2** _(asked for and got late)_ _**Having asked three times between March and July,** I received the code in September._ **Subject: I. I asked. ✓**',
  '**3** _(measured twice)_ _**Having measured the shelf on the ninth and the eleventh,** I can give you one figure and not a range._ **Subject: I. ✓**',
  '**4** _(a decision somebody else took)_ ⚠ _**The committee having declined the second application,** we have changed the ask._ **Note the shape: when the subject of the participle is not yours, it goes inside the construction** — _the committee having declined_ — **and the main clause keeps its own subject.**',
  '**5** _(a passive participle)_ _**Having been reviewed in November,** the file was returned without a recommendation._ ⚠ **Correctly built and hiding two people: reviewed by whom, returned by whom?** **Both are absent and both were in the room.**',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark these on **the subject test and nothing else**, and insist on the test being written: circle the subject, draw a line to the participle. **Item 4 is the one worth teaching explicitly** — ⚠ **it is the repair for every broken sentence in the cycle**, and almost nobody arrives at it alone: **when the doer of the participle is not the subject of your main clause, put the doer inside the construction.** **Item 5 is correctly built, and the discussion is whether its author meant to hide anybody.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**A** _(reads)_ _Having been refused twice, we changed the ask._',
  '**B** Subject of the main clause: **we.**   Did it do the participle? **Yes.**',
  '**B** ⚠ _(the second row)_ _Having considered the matter, the start of the day will move._ **Subject: the start of the day. No.** **It could be _the governors_ or _the head_ — and the difference is whether a parent is reading a decision of one man\'s or of a committee\'s.**',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The ten main-clause subjects, and the six that did not do the participle. ⚠ **Five of the six are the ordinary fault. The sixth is _having been assessed in November, the three words have never gone_ — and it is doing something the other five are not.** **The three words were not assessed; she was.** **But the sentence as written makes the three words the thing with a history, and the writer disappears from her own account of being assessed.** ⚠ **Put the corrected version beside it:** _having been assessed in November, I have never stopped hearing three words._ **Ask the room which is more comfortable to write.** **It is the broken one, every time, and that is why it was written.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _**Having been turned down for the registration file twice on the same paperwork,** I have asked the council what evidence they accept rather than sending the same evidence again._ ⚠ _Subject: **I**. Line drawn to **having been turned down**. **I was turned down. ✓**_',
  '**b** _(partner)_ _**My first version was: having been turned down twice, the paperwork has been resubmitted.** She wrote the two subjects a translator would have to choose between — **the council** or **me** — and said she thought I meant me. ⚠ **I did, and I had written it so that nobody could tell**, which is a thing I shall look for from now on in letters about my own mistakes._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Rizk brought three applications for the same thing: two refused and the third funded, and the difference is about nine words. The second said we have applied twice before, we are applying again because the need has not changed. Mrs Tarabishi said that invites the obvious answer, and it invites the answer they had already given twice — because the history was in the main clause, so the history was the news, and the news was that she was applying again, which is not news to anybody. The third said: having been refused twice on the same ground, we have changed the ask and not the argument. The history is in front, where it cannot be answered, and the main clause carries the one thing that is actually new. Mr Shihabi said that is a lead, that is exactly what a lead is, and Mrs Rizk said then she has been writing eleven years of second paragraphs. Mr Tayara then brought four of his own, thinking three were broken: having considered the matter carefully, the start of the day will move to twenty past eight. Mrs Khoury said the start of the day did not consider anything. No — he did, and he has written that sentence, or one like it, about forty times. Mrs Oueidat said she sees this constantly and cannot translate it: there is no subject for the participle, so rendering it into Arabic means inventing one, and whichever one she invents puts somebody in the document who was not in it. Mr Tayara asked her to say more, and if she supplies the school, the school considered it; if she supplies the head, he did — two different documents, and the English version does not choose, which is the first thing counsel reaches for. Mr Zahreddine put the test as: who is the subject of the main clause, and did that person do the participle? And Mr Tayara gave the finding he was taking away: when the participle does not belong to the subject, a reader cannot find who did it, and a reader who cannot find who did it assumes you did not want them to. Dr Qabbani said his are all passive for exactly that reason — having been reviewed, the data were re-analysed — and Mrs Khoury said that one is correctly built and still hiding somebody, because the data were reviewed by whom, and correct and invisible are not the same as correct and honest. Mrs Ajouz asked how many can go in one letter, and two: three becomes a tic and a tic is visible. Mrs Rizk\'s third application has two, her fourth draft had five, and the fifth reads like somebody doing an exercise.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the two subjects a translator would choose between. ⚠ **Collect every pair and look for one pattern: how many of the broken sentences are about the writer\'s own mistake?** **It is usually most of them.** **That is the finding of the cycle arriving personally: the dangling participle is not distributed at random through a person\'s writing** — **it clusters where the writer did not want to be the subject.** **Say it once, here, and then let the homework prove it on four sentences each.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Having considered the matter, the start of the day will move._ → **✓** _..., I am moving the start of the day._   **✗** _Having reviewed the file, the application was declined._ → **✓** _the committee having reviewed the file, we have declined…_   **✗** Five participle clauses in one letter. → **✓** two.   **✗** A pause after the comma. → **✓** one breath.   **✗** _Having waited eleven months, the code appeared._ → **✓** _having waited eleven months, I was given the code._'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **It puts the two refusals where they cannot be answered and leaves the main clause for the one thing that is new.** **As a separate sentence — _we have applied twice before_ — the history is the news, and a reader who already has the file knows it.** **The main clause then carries _we have changed the ask and not the argument_, which is the only sentence in the document the reader has not already seen.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **Eighty-two self-presentations across four districts**, given in February. **It was true and useless because it did not separate the districts** — and _the district column having been in the book all along and not having been read_ **is her own admission inside the sentence that corrects it.** **Separated, it is 51 · 14 · 11 · 6, and the 51 is the whole case.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **The ground in one sentence, with the district column mentioned in it**, so that a fourth submission either answers it or is not made. **The reason is a cost on both sides: about four hours of the assessor\'s time and about three weeks of hers.** **Note what she does not do: she does not ask them to approve it, and she does not complain about the two refusals.** **She asks for the one thing that would make a fourth application either possible or unnecessary.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
