out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Two readings of one paragraph, both by people who learned English the way this class did. Asked which reader is right, Mrs Oueidat said neither: the first has marked it as a classmate and the second as an editor, and the writer did not ask either of them for that. Mr Shihabi said the second reader is nearly right and is right for the wrong reason — sentences three and four do indeed do nothing, but cutting them is a length argument, and the writer will cut them and still be writing B2 paragraphs in June. Mrs Tarabishi asked what is actually wrong with it, and nothing is wrong with it: it is correct and it is visible, and the visible part is six words long, the reason is that. Dr Qabbani said six words that announce a reason is coming, and six words that put the reason in its own sentence, where the customer can take it and argue with it on its own — and the customer will, because eleven degrees over is exactly the kind of fact somebody rings up about. Mrs Rizk said she has written that sentence, for seven weeks, about four pallets. Mr Tayara asked for the joined version, and Mrs Khoury gave it: we are unable to supply the four pallets ordered on 2 September, the cold store having been eleven degrees over since the eighteenth of August. One sentence, the fact inside it, and nothing to telephone about except the pallets. Then Mrs Ajouz said they have been told all year not to tell people what to do with their own writing, which is the whole cycle, and Mr Zahreddine said then do not tell her anything: show her her own two sentences joined, and stop. Asked what to do if she asks what was wrong with hers, he said to say that nothing was wrong with it, which is true, and that the joined one cannot be answered, which is also true and is the only thing in it she can check for herself. Then Mr Attar said what he would have wanted said to him, because he was that writer in June and somebody did tell him and he did nothing about it: do not give her the rule — give her her own sentence and one question, what would the customer telephone about now? He was given a rule, kept the rule and lost the paper. Mrs Khoury put the mediation as three moves: say what is correct and mean it; join her own two sentences in front of her and stop; ask what is left to telephone about. And Mrs Oueidat gave the thing not to do, which both readers did: do not rank the sentences. Sentences three and four do nothing is a verdict on a document she is going to send tomorrow, and a writer who has been ranked defends the ranking instead of reading the joined sentence.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the student\'s own six visible words and their joined version. ⚠ **Collect the six-word phrases and count the distinct ones.** **There are rarely more than five in a room of ten**, and that is the most encouraging finding in the unit: **the visible prose of ten working adults in four professions is made of about five phrases.** **Write them up and leave them up for the rest of Book 1.** **Then the part that matters more than the list: ask how many people, doing move 2 on a partner, managed to say nothing after the joined sentence.** **It is about half**, and the other half talked through the silence — **which is the same fault as filling the gaps in 1.6, arriving in a conversation instead of on a page.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Sentences three and four do nothing._ → **✓** do not rank her sentences.   **✗** _Put the reason inside the news sentence._ → **✓** no rule: give her the joined sentence.   **✗** _There is nothing wrong with it._ → **✓** true, and silent about the reader.   **✗** _It should be one and a half sentences._ → **✓** a length argument.   **✗** Talking through the silence after move 2. → **✓** stop, and count.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read – exam practice (Reading and Use of English Part 3)'));
out.push(A('**1 NOTICEABLE   2 VISIBILITY   3 OBSERVATION   4 HABITUAL   5 CONCEALMENT   6 APPARENTLY   7 DETECTABLE   8 SUBORDINATION**'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Part 3 is answered from the slot and not from the meaning, and the slot is settled by the words on either side of the gap.** **Teach the two-second routine: read the words immediately before and after, decide the part of speech, then build it.** **_almost no ___ features_ wants an adjective before a noun — _noticeable_, and not _noticed_, which cannot sit there.** **_a degree of ___ wants a noun — _visibility_.** **_escapes ___ wants a noun after a verb — _observation_.** **_something ___ wants an adjective after _something_ — _habitual_, where _habitually_ cannot follow it.** **_amounts to ___ wants a noun — _concealment_.** **_an ___ artless sentence_ wants an adverb modifying an adjective — _apparently_.** **_finds ___ wants an adjective as object complement — _detectable_.** **_the grammar of ___ wants a noun — _subordination_.** ⚠ **Every one of the eight is fixed by the grammar before the meaning is considered at all**, and a class told this in Unit 1 will stop losing marks to plausible wrong forms.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
