out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**Of the three claims in the article, the two worth taking seriously are the first and the third, because they are the only two that can be tested against something.** The second — that experts misjudge their own competence — is probably true and is unanswerable as stated, there being no way to assess the assessor without an infinite regress. The first and third, by contrast, make predictions.',
  '**The claim that good work looks easy and is therefore undervalued is half right, and the half that is wrong matters more.** Work that looks easy is certainly harder to charge for: a translation nobody notices, a frame that is simply certified, a letter that produces no telephone call. But the claim assumes that visible effort is rewarded, and it is not. **A document that advertises its own care is read as defensive, and a reader who can see the method begins to have opinions about it.** The penalty is not that easy-looking work is undervalued; it is that both kinds of work are judged by readers who cannot see effort at all.',
  '**The third claim is the one I would defend without qualification, and it explains the first.** Training has to make a method visible, because an invisible method cannot be copied. **The consequence is that every competent person passes through a stage of producing prose that announces its own structure**, and some stay there, not because they have stopped learning but because nobody has told them that the scaffolding comes down.',
  '**What follows is a practical conclusion rather than an opinion.** The stage of visible method is not a fault to be avoided but a condition to be left, and the only reliable sign of having left it is that other people stop being able to describe how the work was done.',
]));
